ual  brain initiative investigator meeting so  i will talk about  our uh recent work on this  single cell transcriptome imaging method  that my lab developed  and our application of this method  to generate a cell analysis of a couple  of brain regions  so first let me give you a motivation of  why we want to develop a single cell  transcriptome energy method  now we know that animals are made up of  many different types of cells  any cells have different behaviors and  functions largely because they have for  different  gene expression profiles and therefore  being able to quantify  the rna expression profile or the  transcriptome of individual cells  could greatly help our understanding of  the molecular origin of different cell  behaviors  and indeed the recent advancing singles  rna sequencing has begun to transform  many areas of biology so then why do we  want to develop a  imaging-based single-star  transcriptomics approach  now uh one of the main reasons  is because imaging-based approaches  allow us to do spatially resolved  measurement  and that is important in at least two  fronts  and now at the cellular level we know  that rnas are not uniformly distributed  inside cells  and the local rna distributions and the  actions are actually important it is an  important post-transformational  regulation form and it is important for  the  establishment and maintenance of local  cellular structures  as described in this beautiful review by  rob  singer and at a tissue level  we also know as i said there are so many  different types of cells  and these cells are also not uniformly  distributed  and the spatial organization of the  different cell types  is actually important for the tissue  function so if we could actually do in  situ single cell gene expression  profiling  then we can identify the cell types in  situ  and then with that directly map out this  functionally  important spatial organization  so how do we do this so here's our idea  we use aerobusted b