  dual projections to both mo's and ssps  do they have unique transcription  signatures  or enriching specific clusters  so uh that's a very good question i can  answer this way so  the we do have observation  of the same cluster of cells  certainly even as the cells that are  very nearby spatially and belong to the  same cluster  can project both to mos and ssp  and do we have observation if the  question  asks for is that is there one particular  cell that exactly cell  actually projected two regions uh i  actually need to  uh further look into the data to to  answer  that question so it's not something that  i know  of the top of my head and do we have  unique transcription signature  uh or enriched in specific  yes so unique may be too strong to say  the projection  as i try to emphasize is a very  interesting pattern that's a  what we call a many to many so we what  we often find is  one target region can receive input from  many clusters  and one cluster can project to many  different target regions  and yet that doesn't mean it's a mess  without any rules so  we do see the specificity of a specific  composition or combination of clusters  that  project to a certain region on top of  that sometimes we even as i alluded to  to have very similar molecularly similar  and spatially similar cells that project  distinctly to two different regions now  we'll give rise to these uh  is what i said you know it's an  interesting question for the future  and uh have you used murfish for uh  doing studies to characterize  astrocytes and their interactions with  neurons  yeah that's another very good question  so uh  in all our uh brain murphy images  there are non-neuronal cells and  certainly astrocytes  and it is a very interesting thing to  dig deeper into these images to examine  the  the astrocytes neuron interaction while  we  currently have focused more on neurons  so but that's some  that's a direction we're interested in  now a question related to the  hypothalamic findings where you are  actually elegantly documenting  differences between males and females  as it relates to parenting rearing but  you only reported data on aggression for  the males  was nothing on the females so was it  because the females did not experience  aggressive behaviors or  or where they're older  yeah so uh that's an interesting  question  so uh uh my collaborator katherine  dulac probably can answer that question  better you know  so we have uh uh obviously  in these studies definitely have males  exposed to pop and males exposed to  adult male we have also  female exposed to pop and female exposed  to  uh to uh to adult male  and in the female exposed to pop case  we see the parenting behavior and the  female exposed to the adult  male at least to what we quantified as  the mating behavior  you know whether there were ever some  incidences of aggression i don't know  who had to ask  her and her postdocs whether they  observed that  yeah that was an interesting question i  mean one that we cannot forgo now that  we're seeing  aggression surrounding us unfortunately  now there's another question that um  came here that you may have answered to  a certain extent  which relates to um first of all they  say this is very exciting work  and they ask can you detect dynamic  changes  or is it a one-time measure  well technically measurements  that one measurement that you do is a  one-time measure right because these are  fixed  cells that are fixed at a specific time  point  and then the measurements you measure  reflect that time point  nonetheless you know dynamic changes  is something that is possible that one  can imagine  two different approaches that we've  actually taken so you could take  different time points  after a certain initial time point or  especially  stimulation you can you know get your  sample at different time points  and then measure or in some cases  we often find that we could do  pseudo-time pseudo timing of the cells  that can also  infer uh dynamic changes for example in  one of our work  what we actually did is pseudo timing  that reflect a cell cycle dependent  changes  uh you know in the what we see  is if we actually this is very  interesting if we actually take  advantage of our spatial resolution we  can resolve  what genes are in the nucleus what genes  are in the cytoplasm you know the rna  composition  and since rnas are born in the nucleus  and then exported on  into the cytoplasm so then if you  actually get the balance between these  two you can actually pseudo time the  cell cycle point  of the of the of the cells and then  there we can actually see a lot of cell  cycle dependent or uh the gene express  a lot of genes their expression levels  depends on cell cycle  now core pseudo timing can be used for  not only that but also for uh  development and and so on  differentiation if you go to  sort of the early days of development of  the animal  so through that kind of a measurement at  different  physical time points or the pseudo  timing when we'll be able to infer this  kind of a dynamic changes  yeah it's incredible work i mean it's  just amazing so  so congratulations and and  and i would you if you as you're looking  at the work uh where do you want to  bring it next to  and what are some of the challenges that  you would like to resolve  yeah so uh  so the the the method after  you know years of development  is uh already at a point that we can use  it  to solve or to tackle a lot of  biological  questions and the brain related question  is  one area that i'm particularly  interested in  but then one could also imagine uh using  this tool  to get the cell atlas of all kinds of  different organs and in different  organisms  one could also use that for disease  related studies and then one thing  that is uh you know one good example for  example is to do  a related studies to look at the tumor  micro environment  to look at the interaction between uh  healthy thousand  tumor cells and uh to look at the  interaction between tumor cells and  immune cells  and then to study the drug responses and  so on  so there are a lot of and then you know  there are also a lot of us  clearly a lot of biological i mean  development related questions  that can be addressed or was this kind  of a tool to look at how cell types  develop  and also cell differentiation questions  and technically indeed there are still  advances you know even though i say that  there's not really a limit necessarily  on how many genes but i did mention some  of the genes  are like the very short genes that are  still difficult to detect and so on  and then there are also interesting  questions that are related to to the  sequence length  for example uh you know  in the the the uh  the the spli the spice variants for  example right  isoforms and so on so so uh there are  really still  it's it's still a relatively new  direction that there are a lot of  technical advances that can still happen  well i want to thank you very much for  that terrific  talk and give you an opportunity is  there anything else that you would have  liked anyone to ask you that you want to  leave us with  i think we have had a lot of questions  already  and then i had already ran over time so  i just want to thank all the people who  has  uh listened to the talk and showed  interest  and you know it's uh it's a great  pleasure to uh  to to present our work in this platform  and  not only i want to thank the audience  but also  uh thank you uh nora so much  for doing this fantastic job monitoring  and  also i'd like to thank the technical  teams  of lab route and the nih to make all  this possible you know we're all  learning on how to do  remote conferencing so uh this is a uh  interesting experience and really a lot  of work from the technical support  people to make this possible so i like  to take this opportunity to thank them  thanks very much
 

